Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);
</code>
In the given code snippet, `New_Ret` is a Boolean variable and it will be false only when x is not found in the list `arrList`. 

The `LastIndexOf` method in C# returns the zero-based index of the last occurrence of a value in the list. If the value doesn't exist in the list, `LastIndexOf` method will return -1. 

According to the problem statement, the precondition is that `x` isn't in the `arrList` when `Return` is `false` and `LastIndexOf(x)` is less than -1. In this program snippet, if `New_Ret` is false, the last index of value x in old list must be greater than or equals to -1. Therefore, the desired assert statement is `Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || New_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);`.

Please note debug.assert is part of .net Framework and not available in .net core due to limited cross-platform capabilities. You might need to adapt the solution according to the platform you develop in.--------------------
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == false && Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);
</code>--------------------
Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || Old_arrListLastIndexOfX >= -1);
</code>

Notice the use of the 'arcely' prefix which should be kept in mind with every assertion. Catch22 debugging can often result from not asserting your assumptions, making it challenging to diagnose the problem.--------------------
